His Excellency further clarified that Cambodia has not deployed any newly laid mines, as alleged by the Thai side
The mines present on Cambodian soil are remnants from the civil war era
His Excellency Ambassador Dara In delivers Cambodia’s statement in the Interactive Dialogue with the Special Rapporteur on the Situation of Human Rights in Cambodia
In his reply intervention, the Ambassador affirmed that while Cambodia welcomes the Rapporteur’s first call to uphold the ceasefire, peace cannot rest on words alone while forced displacement, encircled villages, property seizures, forced evictions and the plight of captured soldiers remain unresolved
Cambodia is not paralysed; it is advancing—rising from war to rebuild institutions of peace and law
Elections were free, civil society vibrant, and progress undeniable
Civil and political freedoms, he reminded, cannot endure where economic and social rights collapse
He therefore urged the Special Rapporteur to uphold the Code of Conduct with impartiality and balance
The Ambassador further called on the Human Rights Council to engage with fairness: recognising achievements as well as challenges, resisting politicisation and double standards and reaffirming cooperation grounded in mutual respect
Cambodia, he underlined, remains steadfast in its commitment—to peace as the foundation of rights, to development as the path to dignity and to the full enjoyment of all human rights for all our people
During the session, several delegations spoke overwhelmingly in favour of Cambodia’s achievements, progress and efforts in advancing the promotion and protection of human rights for all
